::ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+2 and g(x)=3x, what is (f+g)(x)? {
= 4x+2#Correct. Add the rules: x+2+3x=4x+2.
~ 3x<sup>2</sup>+2#This multiplies instead of adding.
~ 4x#This omits the constant 2.
~ 3x+2#This is f(g(x)), not f+g.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x<sup>2</sup> and g(x)=x+1, what is f(g(x))? {
~ x<sup>2</sup>+1#This adds 1 after squaring instead of substituting.
= (x+1)<sup>2</sup>#Correct. Substitute the entire expression x+1 into f.
~ x<sup>2</sup>(x+1)#This multiplies the functions.
~ 2x+1#This is not the composition.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=2x and g(x)=x-5, what is g(f(3))? {
~ -4#This applies g to 3 first only.
~ 6#This stops after f(3).
= 1#Correct. First f(3)=6, then g(6)=1.
~ 11#This adds instead of subtracting 5.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>For (f/g)(x), what values must be excluded? {
~ Values where f(x)=0#The numerator may be zero.
~ All positive values#Positive values are not automatically excluded.
~ All real values#Only denominator zeros are excluded.
= Values where g(x)=0#Correct. The denominator function cannot equal zero.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x-4 and g(x)=5x, what is f(g(x))? {
= 5x-4#Correct. Substitute 5x into f.
~ 5x(x-4)#This multiplies the functions.
~ x-20#This substitutes incorrectly.
~ 5x+4#The sign of the constant is wrong.
}

